## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint—a fundamental DP pattern in scheduling and resource allocation. It also tests the ability to reconstruct the actual solution, not just compute the optimal value.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with an exact cardinality constraint
- Precomputing p(i) via binary search after sorting intervals by their right endpoint
- Two-dimensional DP table indexed by interval position and number selected
- Backtracking through the DP table to recover the chosen intervals

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKIntervals(intervals, A, k) {
    intervals.sort((a, b) => a.r - b.r);
    const n = intervals.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const selected = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        const [l, r] = [intervals[i-1].l, intervals[i-1].r];
        const v = intervals[i-1].value;
        const p = findPrev(intervals, i-1);

        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            if (dp[p][t-1] + v > dp[i-1][t]) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + v;
                selected[i][t] = true;
            } else {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
        if (selected[i][t]) {
            result.unshift(i - 1);
            t--;
            i = p;
        } else {
            i--;
        }
    }

    return { maxValue: dp[n][k], indices: result };
}

function findPrev(intervals, idx) {
    let lo = 0, hi = idx;
    while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2);
        if (intervals[mid].r <= intervals[idx].l) lo = mid;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Preprocessing p(i): O(n log n) via sorting and binary search
- DP computation: O(n × k)
- Reconstruction: O(n + k)
- Space: O(n × k) for the DP table

## Why This Is Asked
Explores a new DP angle: subarray-level min as value, exact k non-overlapping blocks, with clear reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact block count
- Precomputation of subarray minima
- Backtracking to obtain endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Assumes A is 1-indexed, n = A.length-1, k given
function maxSumMinSubarrays(A, n, k) {
  const M = Array.from({length: n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for (let l=1; l<=n; l++) {
    let m = A[l];
    for (let r=l; r<=n; r++) {
      m = Math.min(m, A[r]);
      M[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const opt = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
      for (let l=1; l<=i; l++) {
        const cand = dp[l-1][t-1] + M[l][i];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; opt[i][t] = l; }
      }
    }
  }
  // backtrack for endpoints
  let i = n, t = k, blocks = [];
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = opt[i][t];
    blocks.push([l, i]);
    i = l-1; t--;
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests comprehensive DP design for partitioning problems with nonlinear segment costs involving both min and sum operations, requiring careful handling of base cases and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-dimensional DP over partition indices
- Range minimum query optimization within transitions
- Complete solution from base cases through reconstruction
- Space-time tradeoffs and optimization strategies

## Code Example
