## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP over partitions where block value depends on both max and sum, plus reconstruction of segments. Encourages discussion on precomputation of range maxima and efficient transitions.

## Key Concepts

- DP over partitioning with fixed number of segments
- Block value using max and sum requires precomputation
- Path reconstruction for exact endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const pref=new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+A[i];
  // precompute max for all subarrays
  const maxSub=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ let m=A[i]; for(let j=i;j<n;j++){ m=Math.max(m,A[j]); maxSub[i][j]=m; } }
  const blockVal=(l,r)=> (r-l+1)*maxSub[l][r] - (pref[r+1]-pref[l]);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t]=[i-1,t];
      for(let s=1;s<=i;s++){
        const val=dp[s-1][t-1] + blockVal(s-1,i-1);
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=[s-1,t-1]; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack from (n,k) using par
  return {best: dp[n][k], par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to avoid O(n^2 k) in practice?  
- How does reconstruction behave with negative block values?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

## Why This Is Asked
Offers a new DP angle: path with exact turns, combining grid path DP with turn accounting. Tests deriving a tight state space, base cases, and reconstruction, mirroring routing-like constraints in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with an extra dimension for turns
- Two direction states to capture turns
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Careful base/edge-case handling (start cell, negative infinities)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  // 4D DP: [i][j][t][d]
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () =>
    Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])));
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        // last move right
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(
            (dp[i][j-1][t][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t][0] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
        // last move down
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(
            (dp[i-1][j][t][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t][1] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][K][0], dp[n-1][m-1][K][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k turns and also maximize path length if grid values included negative numbers?
- How would you optimize space to O(nmK) memory or better, and what would be the trade-offs for reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP with a constrained path problem and reconstruction, a common real interview topic for large-scale environments like PayPal or Cloudflare.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP state: i, j, t, dir (0 right, 1 down)
- Turn counting via transition from one dir to the other
- Base cases for first row/column
- Path reconstruction pointers and memory/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) dp[0][j][0][0] = dp[0][j-1][0][0] + grid[0][j];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) dp[i][0][0][1] = dp[i-1][0][0][1] + grid[i][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromLeftSame = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromLeftTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromLeftSame, fromLeftTurn));
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromTopSame = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromTopTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromTopSame, fromTopTurn));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestDir = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
      if (dp[n-1][m-1][t][d] > best) {
        best = dp[n-1][m-1][t][d]; bestT = t; bestDir = d;
      }
    }
  }
  return {best, bestT, bestDir};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the path coordinates efficiently?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n*m*k) or O(k) with rolling arrays?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests precise DP construction for selecting k disjoint subarrays with per-subarray penalty, a common optimization variant.

## Key Concepts
- Disjoint subarray DP with two states
- Transition derivation for starting/continuing a subarray
- Space/time trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  best[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], t>0 ? best[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] : NEG);
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t], end[i][t] - c);
    }
  }
  return {value: best[n][k], subs: null};
}
```

